1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,800
In episode one, two agents worked together. But they passed plain text, and the second agent always ran.

2
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:14,345
Today, you'll turn them into a real pipeline: typed data between agents, and a check in plain Java that can stop early.

3
00:00:14,795 --> 00:00:18,529
It's the most common multi-agent pattern, and the easiest to get right.

4
00:00:20,079 --> 00:00:21,483
So, what is an agent pipeline?

5
00:00:21,933 --> 00:00:25,083
Agents run in a fixed order, like an assembly line.

6
00:00:25,533 --> 00:00:28,178
Each agent's output becomes the next agent's input.

7
00:00:28,628 --> 00:00:33,069
Every step has one small job, so every step is easier to get right.

8
00:00:33,519 --> 00:00:38,659
And between steps, you can add gates: plain Java checks that decide if the line continues.

9
00:00:40,109 --> 00:00:41,855
The key idea is typed data.

10
00:00:42,305 --> 00:00:47,979
If an agent answers in free text, your code can't easily check it. You'd have to parse sentences.

11
00:00:48,429 --> 00:00:52,761
With structured outputs, Claude's answer must follow a JSON schema you give it.

12
00:00:53,211 --> 00:01:00,532
And in Java, you don't even write the schema. The SDK builds it from a record, and parses the answer back into that record.

13
00:01:00,982 --> 00:01:04,809
So you can check the data with normal Java code. No extra LLM call.

14
00:01:06,259 --> 00:01:07,463
When should you use a pipeline?

15
00:01:07,913 --> 00:01:13,180
When the steps are known in advance, always in the same order, and you can check each result.

16
00:01:13,630 --> 00:01:20,696
If the next step depends on what you find, that's routing. That's the next episode. And if one prompt already works, keep it simple.

17
00:01:22,146 --> 00:01:24,798
We'll upgrade the code-review team from episode one.

18
00:01:25,248 --> 00:01:31,965
Before: the Reviewer wrote free text notes, and the Writer always ran, even when there was nothing to fix.

19
00:01:32,415 --> 00:01:42,377
After: the Reviewer returns a Review record. A gate in Java drops the low-severity bugs, and stops if nothing serious is left. The Writer only runs when it's needed.

20
00:01:43,827 --> 00:01:45,348
Here's the team from episode one.

21
00:01:45,798 --> 00:01:50,590
The Reviewer returns a string. My code can't tell if it found zero bugs or ten.

22
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:54,415
So the Writer always runs, and always costs tokens.

23
00:01:55,865 --> 00:01:59,616
Step one: typed answers. I create Review dot java.

24
00:02:00,066 --> 00:02:02,879
A review has a short summary, and a list of bugs.

25
00:02:05,729 --> 00:02:09,971
Each bug has a line number, a severity, the problem, and the fix.

26
00:02:10,421 --> 00:02:16,981
Severity is an enum: low, medium or high. Claude can only answer with one of these three values.

27
00:02:17,431 --> 00:02:21,077
That's the contract between the two agents. Plain Java records.

28
00:02:22,427 --> 00:02:27,150
Step two: upgrade the Agent class. The top is the same as in episode one.

29
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:33,356
Now the new part: a run method with a type. You give it a class, and you get an object of that class back.

30
00:02:36,872 --> 00:02:46,036
One line does the magic: output config with the type. The SDK turns the record into a JSON schema, and Claude's answer must match it.

31
00:02:46,486 --> 00:02:52,044
The loop is the same as before. Structured outputs work together with tools in the same request.

32
00:03:03,114 --> 00:03:08,586
When Claude is done, I don't return text anymore. I return the typed object, already parsed.

33
00:03:09,036 --> 00:03:17,607
And the old run method still works. It asks for a small Answer record, and returns its text. So text agents, like our Writer, don't change.

34
00:03:18,957 --> 00:03:22,522
Step three: the gate. In ReviewPipeline dot java.

35
00:03:22,972 --> 00:03:26,491
The gate is plain Java. No LLM, no tokens.

36
00:03:28,822 --> 00:03:34,738
It keeps only the bugs that are not low severity. Because the review is typed, it's one simple filter.

37
00:03:35,188 --> 00:03:39,244
And because it's a normal method, I can unit test it without calling the API.

38
00:03:40,594 --> 00:03:45,092
Step four: build the pipeline. The two agents are the same as in episode one.

39
00:03:47,942 --> 00:03:52,518
Step one of the pipeline: the Reviewer returns a Review object, not a string.

40
00:03:55,470 --> 00:03:59,522
Here I pass Review dot class. That's the only change for a typed agent.

41
00:03:59,972 --> 00:04:05,732
Then the gate. If no serious bugs are left, the pipeline stops here. The Writer is never called.

42
00:04:06,182 --> 00:04:11,316
Otherwise, the Writer gets only the serious bugs. Less input, cleaner context.

43
00:04:12,666 --> 00:04:16,743
Let's test it. First, an offline check. No API key needed.

44
00:04:17,193 --> 00:04:21,877
The SDK accepts our records as a schema, and the gate works on fake reviews.

45
00:04:22,327 --> 00:04:25,216
Now the real pipeline, on the buggy user service.

46
00:04:25,666 --> 00:04:28,501
Serious bugs, so the gate let the Writer run.

47
00:04:28,951 --> 00:04:31,656
Now a clean file: a small price calculator.

48
00:04:32,951 --> 00:04:38,103
Nothing serious, so the pipeline stopped at the gate. The Writer never ran, and cost nothing.

49
00:04:39,653 --> 00:04:41,033
Four tips for your pipelines.

50
00:04:41,483 --> 00:04:46,216
One: type every handoff. A record between agents, not free text.

51
00:04:46,666 --> 00:04:51,637
Two: write your gates in plain Java. They're free, fast, and easy to test.

52
00:04:52,087 --> 00:04:57,987
Three: pass only what the next agent needs. Our Writer gets the serious bugs, not the whole review.

53
00:04:58,437 --> 00:05:02,295
And four: test each step offline, with fake inputs.

54
00:05:03,845 --> 00:05:04,499
Let's recap.

55
00:05:04,949 --> 00:05:08,395
A pipeline is agents in a fixed order, each with one job.

56
00:05:08,845 --> 00:05:13,743
Pass typed data between them: a Java record, and output config with its type.

57
00:05:14,193 --> 00:05:18,776
And gates in plain Java check each step, stop early, and save tokens.

58
00:05:20,226 --> 00:05:29,451
The full Java project, with the offline check, is linked in the description. Next episode: routing, where an agent decides which specialist gets the job.

59
00:05:29,901 --> 00:05:36,186
If this helped, like the video, and subscribe to Zero Cost AI so you don't miss episode three. See you there!

